<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="537648e5">A Context Diagram provides a clear logical scope boundary between the central process or system object
representing the area to be studied and external entitities or processes. Data flows to and from the central object show
how data and information are shared between the area of study and external agents outside the area of study. During
subsequent analysis, each data flow provides a clue to the analyst of the business events and use cases that exist within
the area of study, and how more detailed analysis efforts may be partitioned.</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="8e294557"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="52805b76"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="d922d802"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="4df44b14">&lt;p&gt;
    To graphically delineate the area of study for an business analysis, process improvement or requirements engineering
    effort and its data flow relationships to external agents (systems, organizations, actors, processes).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Context diagrams also indicate the integration needs of the proposed solution with external systems, organizations and
    processes.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="5eec6ebd"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="4bca33d6"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="d89d264e">&lt;p&gt;
    A Context Diagram may contain the following attributes:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Activity (A single Process that defines the scope of the area to be studied for discovery of requirements and
        improvements)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Artifacts (External agents: Systems, other processes, organizations, entities and roles that interact with the area
        of study but are not included in the scope of the analysis effort)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Data, Information or message flows (Data flows to or from the process (area of study) and the external entities
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Data stores (logical data stores that interact with the area of study via data flows)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="representationOptions" nodeId="2d0d5ecd">&lt;p&gt;
    Yourdon notation for context diagram show below:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
     &lt;img alt=&quot;Context Diagram - Yourdon&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.gen.common.base/workproducts/resources/yourdon_context.png&quot; width=&quot;744&quot; height=&quot;574&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Other notations include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Ganes and Sarson
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Information Engineering (James Martin and Clive Finkelstein)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
